  <!-- TRANSLATOR'S SUMMARY -->
  <div class="tb">
    <div class="tl">&#10022; Translator&rsquo;s Summary &mdash; Halacha Three</div>
    <p>The longest halacha so far (six sections). Based on Likutay I:5, it reads <em>Chalah</em> through a third, entirely new lens: <em>matzah</em> vs. <em>chametz</em>, the sanctification of the <em>mochin</em>, thunderclaps, and the joy of the mitzvah.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Section 1 (&#x05D0;) &mdash; The General Principle:</strong> The supreme goal: perform each mitzvah with such joy that no World-to-Come reward is desired &mdash; only another mitzvah (<em>sechar mitzvah mitzvah</em>). This requires straightness of heart, which requires the crookedness of the heart (<em>akmimiyas shebalev</em>) to be stripped by thunderclaps = powerful prayer. Thunderclaps require the <em>mochin</em> to be cleared of <em>chametz</em> (external wisdoms, desires, stray thoughts), and <em>gevuros</em> to be joined with <em>chasadim</em> (left within right). The right side alone makes the mind white as silver.</li>
      <li><strong>Section 2 (&#x05D1;) &mdash; Bread, Matzah, Mochin, and the Entire Year:</strong> Bread = <em>sa&rsquo;ada d&rsquo;liba</em> = the medium through which the <em>mochin</em> are drawn (<em>&ldquo;eat, beloved ones&rdquo;</em> = the two inseparable companions = the two aspects of <em>mochin</em>). Holy eating = <em>matzah</em> mode = whole <em>mochin</em> = straight heart = joy. Ten sanctities of bread = ten levels of <em>mochin</em>. <em>HaMotzi</em>: enunciate the <em>heh</em> = transform <em>chametz</em> (ches) into <em>matzah</em> (heh) at every meal. Pesach heals the <em>mochin</em> so deeply that even <em>chametz</em> the rest of the year can be eaten in holiness. Torah given only to eaters of manna (= <em>matzah</em>). Joy of Shavuos = joy of <em>na&rsquo;aseh v&rsquo;nishma</em>. Yoel 2:26: eat and be satisfied &#x2192; praise &#x2192; thunderclaps &#x2192; wonders.</li>
      <li><strong>Section 3 (&#x05D2;) &mdash; Why Water Triggers Chalah (from this angle):</strong> Water brings dough into the scope of leavening = <em>chametz</em>/death-side enters. At this precise moment: give <em>Chalah</em> to Kohen = draw <em>chesed</em> = right = pure mind. Kohen = Avraham = <em>ish hachesed</em> = right. Bread itself grows from thunderclaps (Tehillim 104). <em>Chalah</em> transforms the dough from <em>chametz</em> to <em>matzah</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>Section 4 (&#x05D3;) &mdash; Shabbos, Chalah, and Holy Eating:</strong> Shabbos eating = manna = <em>matzah</em> mode. The two <em>matzos</em> (tablecloths) surrounding the Shabbos loaves recall the manna in its box. The essential <em>oneg Shabbos</em> = feeling World-to-Come joy <em>in</em> the mitzvah itself. This joy flows from <em>Chalah</em>/Kohen/right/<em>matzah</em>/thunderclaps. Hence: bake Jewish bread every Shabbos specifically in order to separate <em>Chalah</em> (Shulchan Aruch, O&rdquo;C 242).</li>
      <li><strong>Section 5 (&#x05D4;) &mdash; Lechem Mishneh, the Exodus, and Alacrity:</strong> Double bread = doubled Torah = Torah doubling its own commandments to teach that every mitzvah is performed for the sake of the next one. The Exodus encodes all this: matzos = mitzvos (al tikri matzos ela mitzvos); expelled from Egypt = flee transgression; <em>&ldquo;could not delay&rdquo;</em> = supreme alacrity; <em>&ldquo;prepared no provisions&rdquo;</em> = no World-to-Come reward sought. The entire Mishnah of Avos 4:2 is encoded in Shemos 12:39. Alacrity = joy; laziness = sadness (same root).</li>
      <li><strong>Section 6 (&#x05D5;) &mdash; Na&rsquo;aseh v&rsquo;Nishma:</strong> <em>Na&rsquo;aseh</em> = performing this mitzvah. <em>Nishma</em> = longing and cleaving (<em>teshukah u&rsquo;deveikus</em>) toward the next mitzvah not yet performed. This is the highest joy &mdash; and why Sages say the crowns of <em>na&rsquo;aseh v&rsquo;nishma</em> represent eternal joy. It is the essential receiving of the Torah.</li>
    </ul>
